  "design": {
    "rooms": 24,
    "replicatesPerSetting": 2,
    "order": "Forward then reversed within each subset. Each entry uses a new room and three independent browser profiles; the host has its own Chromium process tree.",
    "baseline": "Released watch: first challenge after ten seconds of watch, retry five seconds later, ten more seconds to final deadline. The first fresh challenge, not the start of the watch, is time zero; actual WebSocket closure is the separate clean-close control.",
    "counterfactual": "Only the isolated Worker fixture adds 30,000 ms to the final wait. No production setting was changed. Repeated trials are matched by condition across fresh rooms, not observations of both policies in the same room.",
    "transport": "network: hold traffic in both directions on an open socket and release in arrival order; not a radio handoff. reply: hold only genuine nonce-matching responses. suspend: SIGSTOP and SIGCONT on all five CDP-listed processes of a dedicated host Chromium, checked by native process state. No machine slept. close: real clean socket close with reconnects blocked for three seconds. absence: isolate the original through the first ordinary received message.",
    "consentAndTiming": "One offer means a distinct authorization observed at the first eligible guest; no authority change occurs until the guest signs acceptance and the independent observer gets host-changed. For temporary interruptions consent is deliberately withheld until restoration. For continuing absence the candidate accepts on seeing the offer. Ordinary receipt is a unique guest message observed on the other guest’s screen after cleanup, not a relay ACK or an inference that ordinary messages were continuously unavailable. No-offer trials also require later original-host chat and the next correct response on the same socket.",
    "limits": [
      "Loopback Worker on one computer; short intentionally selected holds, two observations per cell, no population false-positive rate, service deadline, physical laptop sleep/wake or mobile/WAN measurements.",
      "New release changes endpoint terminal erasure, not the reachability detector. The comparative timing study deliberately uses the previously deployed browser assets; SHA-256 identifies every input."
    ]
  },
